    "title": "Black hole",
    "pillar": "science",
    "explanation": "A black hole is an astronomical body so compact that its gravity prevents anything, including light, from escaping. Its boundary of no return is the event horizon; crossing it traps objects without any locally detectable change.\n\nIn general relativity black holes contain a central singularity where spacetime curvature becomes infinite. They typically form when very massive stars collapse, and later grow by absorbing matter or merging with other black holes; supermassive ones sit at galactic centers.\n\nThey matter because they power bright accretion disks and quasars, emit gravitational waves when they merge, and—according to quantum theory—slowly lose mass via Hawking radiation, so astronomers infer them from emitted light, nearby star motions, and gravitational waves.",
